    "question": {
      ":question/id": "276c1c1e-88d5-58c4-8c51-085879899419",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186133881/assets/4d5427f3e1c15396ce13bc56438780474464438df6105c63c43773f53cf27495.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- **II.** $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- **III.** $F$ is irrotational\n- **IV.** $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that $F$ represents the velocity field of flowing water. Place a paddlewheel at $P$ with its axis in the $z$-direction. If it rotates, the field has nonzero curl; if it does not, the field has zero curl.\n\nThe arrows produce no local paddlewheel rotation. Thus $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ throughout space. The field is irrotational, and its zero curl points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nWith that in mind, check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n- Statement II is false.\n- Statement III is true.\n- Statement IV is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
